#Generic PDF Processor

This project is a microservice that is essentially just an empty PDF Processor, it takes a PDF File through a REST API Post request and does nothing to it except it writes a "Processed" in Red over each page in the PDF file.

The main objective of this project is to understand the concept of modern microservices and the best practices used in the industry to package and deploy them.

This Repo is basically a masterclass in DevOps and its best practices ...

##Phase #1: Dockerization

In this phase, a production ready and secure dockerfile image is created for the generic-pdf-processor module.
The image should have the ability to have three targets: prod, dev, debug.
the prod tag should be lightweight as much as possible and with least attack surface, while the dev and debug should offer maximum versatility for developers with quality of life improvements for quick developing.


##Phase #2: Scalable Deployment
After building the docker images, what's next is using kubernettes to deploy a scalable appication for the processor, where a gateway of sort will load balance incoming requests on processor instances. In addition, automatically scaling up and down the cluster depending on the workload and requests.

##Phase #3: Monitored Solution
Using Ganfara to monitor key performance metrics of the deployment and incoming traffic. 

##Phase #4: Cloud Deployments
Running kubernettes cluster locally was a step torwards complete cloud adoption, 
this phase will be split into to, Bare Metal AWS EC2 instance and Fully managed AWS Services. The output of both phases will be saved, where two deployments will be present thourghout the project.

###Phase #5.A: AWS EC2 Bare Metal 
this phase will not get into the cloud providers obscure and extensive managed services for deploying applications, it will only use simple vm instances and deploy solutions directly on them.
The catch is that Ansible will be used to configure said instances. AWS EC2 instance will be used only.

###Phase #5.B: AWS Managed ECS/EKS Cloud Deployments
Beaking into cloud realm slowly and abstracting many of Linux Adminstration tasks previously by using fully managed AWS services for hosting and deploying containerized applications.

##Phase #6: Github Automation
The repo shall be pushed to Github where the realm of Github Actions' Workflows is entered. The repo shall offer CI pipeline for automatically running tests and building the image and pushing it to a github package. From which, CD pipelines will follow, remotely deploying the last published images to both deployment A and B.

##Phase #7: Automatic provisioning
The final stretch run of the project, using Terraform to automatically provision both A and B deployments.

##Phase #8: Extend Solution
Use the available infrastructure to extend the solution, adding more use cases and features.


